course: dictionary
lesson: 13177
title: Frayed: worn at the edges
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Frayed is an adjective, and the verb fray tells you how it happens. When the edge of cloth or rope is rubbed and worn over time, the threads start to come loose and stick out. «Her jeans were frayed at the bottom from walking to school.» The bottom of the jeans is worn, with loose threads, so frayed means old and worn.
explain: Frayed can also describe feelings. People can have frayed nerves or frayed tempers when they have been under pressure for too long. «By the end of the game, tempers were frayed.» Nobody's clothes were torn here. The people were worn out and getting cross quickly, so frayed means worn out and easily upset.
explain: Frayed electric cables are dangerous, because the wires inside can be exposed. «The frayed cable could give you a shock, so tell an adult.» If you see a frayed cable or a frayed climbing rope, stop using it straight away and tell an adult.
fact: Frayed means having loose threads at the edge, usually because something is old or worn.
fact: «Frayed» can also mean worn out or easily upset, as in «frayed nerves» and «tempers frayed».
fact: «Fray» is the verb, and «frayed» is its past form: fray, frays, frayed, fraying.
fact: A frayed electric cable can give you a shock or start a fire, so never use it. Tell an adult.
fact: A frayed climbing rope must be replaced, because it is not strong enough to hold you safely.
exercise: «Her jeans were frayed at the bottom.» What does frayed mean here? | Wet and muddy | Very long and wide | Brand new and shiny | *Old and worn, with loose threads at the edge
exercise: «By the end of the game, tempers were frayed.» What does frayed mean here? | The players were fast asleep | *People were cross and upset easily | The game was played with a rope | Everyone was laughing and happy
exercise: «The frayed cable could give you a shock.» What should you do? | Wrap it in tape and keep using it | Keep using it carefully | *Stop using it and tell an adult | Plug it in more tightly
test: «Dad said the old rope was too frayed to climb on.» What does frayed suggest about the rope? | *Old and worn, with loose threads | Brand new and strong | Painted bright red | Very heavy and thick
test: «After a noisy day, Mum's nerves were frayed.» What does frayed nerves mean? | *Mum was worn out and got upset easily | Mum was calm and relaxed | Mum had lost her keys | Mum had a cut on her hand
exam: «Her shoelaces were frayed, so she tied them in a new knot.» What does frayed mean? | Very long and tangled | Brand new | Shiny and clean | *Worn, with loose threads at the end
